    "https://www.westminster.edu/about/community/hr/pdf/Assistant Professor of Computer Science ad 8.2026 - ac.pdf": {
      "title": "• Demonstrated commitment to excellence and inclusivity in undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts",
      "description": "The Westminster College Department of Mathematics and Computer Science invites applications for an \nAssistant Professor of Computer Science, to begin August 2027. We seek a dynamic and innovative teacher-\nscholar who is committed to excellence in undergraduate education and student-centered learning.  \nResponsibilities:  \n• Teach three courses per semester (in person) in a broad range of courses in the computer science \nprogram, the College’s core curriculum, and the first-year program. \n• Maintain and expand our current offerings in Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning.  \n• Maintain an active program of scholarship that engages undergraduate students. \n• Advise and mentor undergraduate students pursuing computer science majors, minors, and \ninterdisciplinary programs. \n• Participate in service to the department, college, and broader academic community.  \n \nQualifications: \n• Ph.D. in Computer Science or Computer Engineering with expertise in Artificial Intelligence or \nMachine Learning by August 2027. \n• Demonstrated commitment to excellence and inclusivity in undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts \nsetting.  \n• Evidence of scholarly productivity and potential for research with undergraduate students.  \n• Given the current political and fiscal climate, Westminster College does not sponsor or take over \nsponsorship of employment Visas at this time. To be hired, applicants must be authorized to work for \nANY employer in the U.S. \n \nWestminster College is a nationally recognized liberal arts college, known for its collaborative environment, \noutstanding experiential learning programs, and focus on student success.  Related to the Presbyterian \nChurch (U.S.A.), Westminster was founded in 1852 as one of the first colleges in the country to offer the \nsame degrees to all people, regardless of gender, race, or religion.  Westminster has been ranked among the \nnation’s top schools for “Top Colleges Doing the Most for the American Dream” (NY Times), “best value for \nthe money” (College Factual), employment success (Educate to Career Index), graduation rate performance \n(U.S. News), social mobility (Washington Monthly), women in STEM disciplines (Forbes), service-learning \n(President’s Higher Education Community Service Honor Roll), and other categories. Around 1,200 \nundergraduate and graduate students benefit from close personal attention from dedicated faculty while \nchoosing from 41 majors, 10 pre-professional programs and 80 organizations.  Westminster’s historic campus \nis located in New Wilmington, PA, a beautiful rural setting near Pittsburgh and Cleveland. \nApplicants should submit via email a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, plan for \nresearch involving undergraduates, and graduate transcripts. Applicants should arrange for three letters of \nrecommendation to be sent, at least one of which must address teaching. All application materials should be \ne-mailed to Dr. Pamela Richardson, Chair, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Westminster \nCollege at cshire@westminster.edu. Complete applications received by October 16, 2026 will receive full \nconsideration, but applications will be accepted after this date until the position is filled.  \nWestminster College is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  "
    },
    "https://www.westminster.edu/about/community/hr/pdf/Visiting Assistant Professor of CS 8.2026 - ac.pdf": {
      "title": "• Demonstrated commitment to excellence and inclusivity in undergraduate teaching and research in a",
      "description": "The Westminster College Department of Mathematics and Computer Science invites applications for a 2-year \nVisiting Assistant Professor of Computer Science, to begin August 2027. We seek dynamic and innovative \nteacher-scholars who are committed to excellence in undergraduate education and student-centered \nlearning.  \nResponsibilities:  \n• Teach three courses per semester (in person) in a broad range of courses in the computer science \nprogram and the College’s core curriculum. \n• Maintain and expand our current offerings.  \n• Maintain an active program of scholarship. \n \nQualifications: \n• Ph.D. in Computer Science or Computer Engineering with expertise in Artificial Intelligence or \nMachine Learning by August 2027. \n• Demonstrated commitment to excellence and inclusivity in undergraduate teaching and research in a \nliberal arts setting.  \n• Evidence of scholarly potential or productivity. \n• Given the current political and fiscal climate, Westminster College does not sponsor or take over \nsponsorship of employment Visas at this time. To be hired, applicants must be authorized to work for \nANY employer in the U.S. \n \nWestminster College is a nationally recognized liberal arts college, known for its collaborative environment, \noutstanding experiential learning programs, and focus on student success.  Related to the Presbyterian \nChurch (U.S.A.), Westminster was founded in 1852 as one of the first colleges in the country to offer the \nsame degrees to all people, regardless of gender, race, or religion.  Westminster has been ranked among the \nnation’s top schools for “Top Colleges Doing the Most for the American Dream” (NY Times), “best value for \nthe money” (College Factual), employment success (Educate to Career Index), graduation rate performance \n(U.S. News), social mobility (Washington Monthly), women in STEM disciplines (Forbes), service-learning \n(President’s Higher Education Community Service Honor Roll), and other categories. Around 1,200 \nundergraduate and graduate students benefit from close personal attention from dedicated faculty while \nchoosing from 41 majors, 10 pre-professional programs and 80 organizations.  Westminster’s historic campus \nis located in New Wilmington, PA, a beautiful rural setting near Pittsburgh and Cleveland. \n \nApplicants should submit via email a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, plan for \nresearch (preferably involving undergraduates), and graduate transcripts. Applicants should arrange for three \nletters of recommendation to be sent, at least one of which must address teaching. All application materials \nshould be e-mailed to Dr. Pamela Richardson, Chair, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, \nWestminster College at cshire@westminster.edu. Complete applications received by October 16, 2026 will \nreceive full consideration, but applications will be accepted after this date until the position is filled.  \nWestminster College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. \n "
    },
    "https://www.westminster.edu/about/community/hr/pdf/CAMPUS STORE MANAGER Posting 9.2026 - ac.pdf": {
      "title": "Position Vacancy",
      "description": "Position Vacancy \nCampus Store Manager \nPosition Summary \nThe Campus Store Manager is responsible for the overall leadership, management, and daily operations \nof the Westminster College Campus Store and remote retail TG2 trailer. This position oversees retail \noperations, inventory management, purchasing, e-commerce, budgeting, employee supervision, and \nvendor relationships to ensure the Campus Store effectively supports the needs of students, faculty, staff, \nalumni, prospective students, and visitors. \nThe Campus Store Manager ensures the availability of college apparel, gifts, office supplies, and other \nmerchandise while creating a welcoming, customer-focused retail environment aligned with the College’s \nmission and operational goals. \nEssential Duties and Responsibilities \n• Direct daily operations of the Campus Store and remote retail trailer to ensure efficient, high-\nquality customer service. \n• Manage the selection, purchasing, and inventory of apparel, gifts, office supplies, and other \nmerchandise. \n• Source and procure college-branded apparel, promotional items, and specialty merchandise. \n• Create designs for unique seasonal merchandise. \n• Prepare purchase orders, requisitions, and vendor communications related to procurement \nactivities. \n• Manage Campus Store websites and e-commerce platforms, including product listings, images, \ncontent updates, FAQs, and time-sensitive communications. \n• Hire, train, schedule, supervise, evaluate, and provide guidance to Campus Store employees and \nstudent workers. \n• Develop staffing schedules and oversee daily operations to maintain appropriate coverage and \nservice levels. \n• Establish and maintain effective relationships with vendors, online retailers, campus \norganizations, and external business partners. \n• Process merchandise returns, prepare shipping documentation, and manage vendor credits. \n• Monitor inventory levels, oversee stockroom organization, and maintain accurate inventory \nrecords. \n• Coordinate annual physical inventory processes and inventory control procedures. \n• Assist with budget management, including invoice approval, cash reporting, accounts receivable \nreporting, and financial documentation. \n• Maintain accurate operational records, reports, and correspondence. \n• Manage commencement regalia operations, including ordering, billing, distribution, returns, and \ncoordination with students, and trustees. \n• Develop merchandising strategies, promotional campaigns, and seasonal displays to increase \nsales and enhance the customer experience. \n• Ensure compliance with college policies, financial procedures, and retail best practices. \n• Perform additional duties as assigned in support of the College’s mission and operational \nobjectives. \nRequired Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities \n• Knowledge of retail management, merchandising practices, and customer service standards. \n• Knowledge of purchasing procedures for educational materials, apparel, promotional products, \nand retail merchandise. \n• Knowledge of inventory management, retail accounting, and business operations. \n• Experience with point-of-sale (POS) systems, cash handling procedures, and retail technology. \n• Ability to troubleshoot and repair hardware and software issues. \n• Knowledge of inventory control processes and annual physical inventory procedures. \n• Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications. \n• Working knowledge of Adobe Photoshop preferred. \n• Experience with ARC Suite, GM2 Merchandise Management, PI Inventory, and INSite web design \nsoftware helpful. \n• Knowledge of UPS shipping procedures and shipping documentation preferred. \n• Strong organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. \n• Excellent written and verbal communication skills. \n• Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and adapt to changing operational needs. \n• Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, faculty, staff, \nvendors, and external partners. \n• Ability to work independently while exercising sound judgment and decision-making. \nQualifications \n• Demonstrated experience managing or supervising retail operations. \n• Experience with purchasing, inventory management, merchandising, and financial processes. \n• Experience hiring, training, coaching, and evaluating employees. \n• Ability to implement new technology systems and improve operational processes. \n• Ability to maintain accurate inventory, financial, and operational records. \n• Ability to develop short-term and long-term goals and implement strategies to achieve \noperational objectives. \n• Ability to provide exceptional customer service in a fast-paced retail environment. \n• Ability to communicate professionally and effectively with diverse campus constituencies. \nPhysical Requirements \n• Ability to lift, carry, move items weighing up to 40 pounds, unpack, and shelve merchandise. \n• Ability to bend, stoop, reach, climb ladders, and perform stockroom activities. \n• Ability to stand and walk for extended periods while assisting customers and operating retail \nsystems. \n• Ability to operate computers, POS equipment, barcode printers, digital signage, and other retail \ntechnology. \n \nWorking Relationships \nThe Campus Store Manager works collaboratively with faculty, department chairs, administrators, \nstudents, student families, staff, alumni, vendors, and external business partners to ensure the Campus \nStore provides exceptional retail services and effectively supports the College’s academic mission. \nInterested Applicants \nQualified applicants should submit a cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional \nreferences to: Gina N. Carrier, Controller, Westminster College, 319 South Market Street, New \nWilmington, PA 16172.  Or via email to carriegn@westminster.edu.   \nApplication materials should be received by October 16, 2026, for full consideration.  Westminster \nCollege is an Equal Opportunity Employer. \n \n \n "
    }
  },
